## Light and dark modes

The theme colour does not change Home Assistant’s light, dark or automatic mode. In dark mode, surfaces are dark neutrals and interactive colours are lighter tints of the theme colour, tested against those surfaces. The header keeps the exact theme colour.

## Colour rules

These rules apply to every colour choice. [Web colours](colour-web.html#colour-roles-in-home-assistant) owns BG-COL-02, BG-COL-04 and BG-COL-05, and [Chart colours](chart-colour.html#bg-col-07) owns BG-COL-07.

<h3 id="bg-col-01">BG-COL-01: Keep the theme colour and identity roles separate</h3>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to theme selection, household personalisation and separate project identity assets.

Use only the seven [GOV.UK theme colours](#theme-colour). Choosing a theme colour must not add a project logo or replace a household name. Keep a family crest or business logo in its own colours and choose a suitable background rather than recolouring it. For example, switching to Green leaves the “Morgan household” title as it is.

civica uses the GOV.UK colour palette. That does not make civica an official government service, and GOV.UK’s identity rules do not apply to civica. The [project identity rules](brand.html) cover civica documentation and promotion. Follow [Personalise your home](../get-started/personalisation.html) for a family name, crest or business logo inside a home.

<%= example('bg-colour-role-separation') %>

<h3 id="bg-col-03">BG-COL-03: Test combinations and retain another cue</h3>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to rendered text, controls and meaningful graphical objects.

Test the real foreground and background pair against the [accessibility requirements](../accessibility/index.html). Normal text needs 4.5:1 contrast and large text needs 3:1. Meaningful control boundaries and graphical objects need 3:1 where the WCAG criterion applies. A quiet divider does not replace a visible control boundary.

Give a status words, a selection a checked or current state, and a chart series a name plus a pattern or shape. For example, show “Front door locked” in text as well as a green icon. Test focus on its real surface, including a dark masthead. A colour-blindness simulation helps a review but does not prove that everyone can tell every pair apart.

<h3 id="bg-col-06">BG-COL-06: Review every display context</h3>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to affected components with every theme colour, in both modes and in accessibility contexts.

The theme colour, and Light or Dark, are independent choices. Use the documented derived dark tokens and keep the exact theme colour on the brand header. Do not make a colour lighter by adding transparency.

Let forced-colour modes use system colours for text, links, focus and controls. Keep borders, labels and symbols if fills disappear, and avoid a blanket `forced-color-adjust: none`. Check real native dialogs, dense settings tables and editors, not only the spacious examples. A documentation preview is not an audit of all 14 combinations (7 theme colours, 2 modes).

<h3 id="bg-col-08">BG-COL-08: Adapt the output channel without importing identity</h3>

**Local requirement: MUST.** Applies to screen, promotional and print colour output.

Dashboard controls need calm colour for repeated use. A release graphic or other social post takes its colours from [Social colours](colour-social.html), and must keep accessible text. Do not use social colours, accent-dot animations or official headers as a second theme in the interface.

For print, treat the supplied CMYK and RAL or descriptive values as references, not measured matches. Use screen hex in sRGB for the application and check a separate proof for printed material. Never change a screen token to approximate a paint or print sample. Illustration colour follows [Illustration colours](colour-illustrations.html).

## Maintain the tokens

The brand source inventory and rule checklist are in `docs/data/brand-guidance-inventory.json` and `docs/data/brand-guidance-checklist.json`. A reviewed source supports a design decision. It is not a pass for an interface. Before closing a checklist item, record the page or component, the theme and mode, the test result and any remaining issue. Check again after a change to a source, palette, component or renderer.

GOV.UK values live in `docs/data/govuk-brand-colours.json`. The theme colours live in `design-system/palettes.json`. After changing either, run `node scripts/generate-themes.mjs` and `python3 docs/scripts/sync-palettes.py` from the repository root, and commit the generated files together. Use `--check` on both to detect drift.

The default theme is `civica`, which uses Blue. The `govuk` entry is a compatibility alias for Blue. Each theme colour has its own theme, named `civica {colour}`.

Every colour section page renders its swatches from `docs/data/govuk-brand-colours.json`, under `pages`. `docs/scripts/generate-social-examples.py` and `docs/scripts/generate-illustration-examples.py` generate the social tiles and the illustrations from that file: if a colour changes, run them again rather than editing the pictures by hand, and use `--check` to find pictures that are out of date. The working OpenStreetMap example takes its colours from `docs/javascript/map-examples.mjs`, and the browser tests check them against the record. Run `npm run check:colours` after the docs build.
